<h1>Rain, sewage and the alert system</h1>
<p class="lede">New Jersey already runs a public alert for combined sewer overflows. It is a real system and it answers a different question from the one a rower is asking.</p>

<h2>What a combined sewer overflow is</h2>
<p>Older sewer systems carry sewage and street runoff in the same pipe. In dry weather everything goes to the treatment plant. In wet weather the pipe fills, and past a certain point the mixture spills out at a relief point into the nearest waterway instead of backing up into basements. That relief point is a combined sewer overflow, and the discharge is untreated or barely treated. NJDEP puts it plainly on the notification system it requires: pollutants in these discharges may make the waterway temporarily unsafe for contact.</p>
<p>Nine permittees share the combined system that drains to the Passaic Valley Sewerage Commission plant in Newark: Bayonne, East Newark, Harrison, Jersey City, Kearny, Newark, North Bergen, Paterson and PVSC itself. They filed one coordinated Long Term Control Plan in October 2020, revised in August 2021, which is the plan for getting the overflows down.</p>

<h2>Where they are, relative to this dock</h2>
<p>Rutherford is not a combined sewer permittee. Nothing on this stretch discharges to our dock from a combined sewer. The permittee upstream of us is Paterson, NJPDES NJ0108880, and its outfalls are above the Dundee Dam, which is the head of tide about four miles up the river. The outfalls below us belong to Newark, Harrison, East Newark and Kearny, downstream of Rutgers Street.</p>
<p>So the wet weather signal at NBC-01 is not one pipe fifty yards away. It is everything the catchment sends down over the dam plus the street runoff that reaches the river here, arriving on the same rain that fills the combined systems upstream and downstream, and moving on a tide that pushes it back and forth past the dock twice a day.</p>
