<h2>What the state system tells you</h2>
<p>Every permittee on this system has to keep a public notification website, and they all use the same one, the NJ CSO Group's CSO Notification System. It shows, by municipality and by waterbody, where a discharge may be happening now or has recently happened, driven by radar rainfall. It also shows the rainfall of the last 24 hours. NJDEP has accepted it as meeting the permit condition. On top of that, the permits require identification signs at the outfalls themselves, carrying the warning that sewage overflows are possible during and after wet weather, contact with water may cause illness, and symbols prohibiting swimming, fishing and kayaking.</p>
<p>What it is: a permit driven notice that a discharge is likely, based on rain. What it is not: a measurement of the water where you are. It does not sample. It cannot tell you that the Passaic at the Nereid dock is at 200 CFU/100 mL this morning and at 2,400 the morning after a storm, because nobody is standing there with a bottle. That is the only thing this project adds.</p>

<h2>How the readings sit beside it</h2>
<p>The two answer different halves of the same question and both are worth having on a Saturday morning.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">CSO Notification System</th><th scope="col">This project</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">What it reports</th><td>a discharge may be occurring, by municipality and waterbody</td><td>an E. coli count from one sample at one dock</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">What drives it</th><td>radar rainfall against permit conditions</td><td>a bottle, a plate and 24 hours in an incubator</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">How current</th><td>within hours, live during a storm</td><td>the count is six days old when the Friday note goes out</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Where</th><td>the whole hydraulically connected system</td><td>40.83 N, 74.11 W, one float</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Cold weather</th><td>notifications are disabled below 35 degrees F</td><td>no sampling in ice, and the season stops in October</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The practical version, which is what goes in the Friday note: if the state system is lit up for the Lower Passaic, believe it and stay off the water regardless of what my last count said. If it is quiet and my last count was red, that is worth knowing too, because a count can be high without a discharge, and street runoff alone will do it.</p>

<h2>The bigger thing this is not about</h2>
<p>The Lower Passaic is a Superfund site, and the contamination that made it one is dioxin and heavy metals in the sediment, not bacteria. Nothing on this site measures any of that, and an E. coli count says nothing about it. If you want that story, it belongs to EPA's Diamond Alkali and Lower Passaic River Study Area records, not to my kitchen table.</p>
